<<<
the verb can only be singular. So Mother or child is (not are) to die. But when the alternatives differ in number, as in Mother or children are to die, Is the child or the parents to be blamed?, the methods in order of merit are: (a) Evade by finding a verb of common number:
===
the verb can only be singular. So *Mother or child is* (not *are*) *to die.* But when the alternatives differ in number, as in *Mother or children are to die, Is the child or the parents to be blamed?*, the methods in order of merit are: (a) Evade by finding a verb of common number:
>>>

<<<
Mother or children must die, Shall the child or the
===
*Mother or children must die, Shall the child or the*
>>>

<<<
parents be blamed?; (b) Invoke ellipsis by changing the order: The mother is to die, or the children, Is the child to be blamed, or the parents? ; (c) Give the verb the number of the alternative nearest it:
===
*parents be blamed?*; (b) Invoke ellipsis by changing the order: *The mother is to die, or the children, Is the child to be blamed, or the parents?*; (c) Give the verb the number of the alternative nearest it:
>>>

<<<
Mother or children are to die, Is the child or the parents to be blamed? What should not be said is Mother or children is to die, Are the child or the parents to be blamed?
===
*Mother or children are to die, Is the child or the parents to be blamed?* What should not be said is *Mother or children is to die, Are the child or the parents to be blamed?*
>>>

